# Study plan: logistic regression vs. kNN on Iris (OpenML 61)

Owner: `story-repo` (plan). Answers `brief.md` requirements R1-R17. Defaults below marked *(default)* may be changed
by the analysis owner (`experiment-repo`) with the reason written in its code/README; everything else is the
objective's and is fixed. No conclusion is presupposed: "no detectable difference" is a valid result.

## Questions

- Q1. What is the expected out-of-sample accuracy of multinomial logistic regression and of kNN (k tuned by inner CV)
  on Iris, each with a 95% interval? (R2-R5)
- Q2. What is the paired accuracy difference (LR - kNN) on identical splits, with a 95% interval; does the interval
  exclude 0? (R6)
- Q3. Is the full nested CV computationally reproducible across machines: on which Ibex node did it run, and are the
  Ibex and local numbers identical? (R7-R10)

## Analyses (deliverer: `experiment-repo`, analysis + presentation)

A1. **Data** (R1). Download OpenML 61 from openml.org (ARFF or OpenML API); verify MD5 `ad484452702105cbf3d30f8deaba39a9`
    for the ARFF (or record the checksum of what was used); store the file once and feed the *same file* to both runs
    (compute nodes may have no network). Features: the 4 numeric columns; target: `class`.

A2. **Outer CV** (R4, R6). `RepeatedStratifiedKFold`-equivalent: 10 repetitions x 5 folds = 50 outer folds, shuffled,
    fixed seed per repetition, recorded. Both methods use the *same* 50 splits (paired).

A3. **Methods** (R2, R3).
    - LR: multinomial (softmax) logistic regression, *(default)* L2, C = 1.0, lbfgs, max_iter high enough to
      converge (record convergence warnings).
    - kNN: *(default)* Euclidean, uniform weights; k chosen per outer training set by inner stratified 5-fold CV
      (*default* seeded, deterministic) over *(default)* odd k in {1, 3, ..., 31}; selection = highest mean inner
      accuracy, ties -> smallest k. Record the chosen k for all 50 outer folds.
    - *(default)* Both inside a pipeline with standardisation fitted on the training part only (same preprocessing
      for both methods; no information from the outer test fold reaches model or k selection).

A4. **Per-fold results** (delivered under `report/results/`): for each of the 50 folds and each method, the number correct out of 30, accuracy, and the
    paired difference; for kNN the chosen k. Written to a machine-readable file (CSV/JSON) that the table, figure and
    every number in the paper are generated from.

A5. **Intervals** (R5, R6). Primary: corrected repeated k-fold CV t-interval (Nadeau & Bengio 2003; Bouckaert &
    Frank 2004): mean +/- t_{0.975, J-1} * sqrt((1/J + n_test/n_train) * s^2), J = 50, n_test/n_train = 30/120.
    Same formula on the 50 paired differences. Secondary (reported for contrast, labelled as such): naive t-interval
    over the 50 folds. If the analysis owner prefers another interval, it states why and keeps the naive one for
    contrast. Note the discreteness (1/30 steps) and many zero differences when interpreting.

A6. **Ibex run** (R7, R9). The full A1-A5 pipeline as one CPU SLURM job (no GPU), job name per the world's
    convention; record `SLURMD_NODENAME`/`hostname`, job id, Python + package versions, CPU model, thread settings,
    and the complete output files. Pin the environment (same package versions as local, e.g. a lock file) and fix
    BLAS/OpenMP thread counts so that a difference, if any, is attributable.

A7. **Local run** (R8). The same code, data file, seeds and pinned versions as a `local-compute` job.

A8. **Identity check** (R10). Compare the two runs' per-fold outputs (correct counts, chosen k, and if feasible the
    predictions) and summary numbers exactly; result = "identical" or a list of differences (where, how much, likely
    cause: versions, BLAS, kNN distance ties). This comparison is itself code delivered with the paper.

## Presentation (deliverer: `experiment-repo`)

- **The one figure** (R14) must support Q1-Q2: per-fold accuracies of both methods over the 50 paired folds (or their
  paired differences), with the means and 95% intervals marked, so a reader can see the spread, the discreteness and
  whether the difference interval covers 0. Vector PDF, legible at NeurIPS single-column width.
- **The one table** (R15): rows LR, kNN, difference (LR - kNN); columns mean accuracy and 95% interval (primary), naive
  interval (secondary) and any short additions that fit (e.g. range of chosen k). Generated by code as a LaTeX
  fragment so its numbers come from the result files. Ibex node name and identical yes/no may be in the table or text.
- Every number the paper prints comes from a file the delivered code writes (R16).

## Paper (deliverer: `paper-repo`, venue + report)

- `report/report.pdf`, compiled from `report/*.tex` with the unmodified `neurips_2026.sty` in submission (default,
  anonymous) mode, at most two pages in total (R11-R13). Exactly one figure and one table, `\input`/`\includegraphics`
  of the files `experiment-repo` delivers (R14, R15). States: data source and checksum, design (10x5 stratified,
  nested k selection, grid, seeds), interval method, results Q1-Q2, Ibex node name and the identity result Q3,
  software versions, where the code is. Cites the sources in `brief.md` section 4 that it uses.
- LaTeX source next to the PDF under `report/` (R17); the build must work from the delivered files.

